                "rob_routing_evidence_json": "[\"This population-based cohort study was conducted in Ontario, Canada, using linked administrative health and demographic data.\", \"We defined the exposed group as patients with diabetes treated with GLP-1 RAs for longer than 6 months. The comparator group (unexposed) was patients with diabetes who never received GLP-1 RAs.\", \"Toincreasethecomparabilitybetweengroups,weused1:2propensity score matching between participants exposed to GLP-1 RA and those who were unexposed.\"]",
                "rob_attempted": true,
                "assessment_status": "completed",
                "rob_skip_or_failure_reason": "",
                "overall_risk_of_bias": "serious",
                "overall_reason": "Overall risk of bias was judged serious for the result on incident neovascular/exudative AMD. The study used a large population-based administrative cohort with propensity-score matching and adjusted Cox models, clear GLP-1 RA exposure groups, and a comparable claims-based outcome definition using AMD diagnosis codes plus ophthalmologist anti-VEGF billing codes. However, as a non-randomized pharmacoepidemiologic study, important residual confounding remains likely: the authors explicitly state that smoking and sun exposure were unavailable, and other potentially relevant confounders for GLP-1 prescribing and AMD risk such as obesity, glycemic control, diabetes severity, diabetic retinopathy, and concurrent diabetes medications were not clearly controlled in the provided text. Selection into the matched analysis and exposure definition also raise concerns because exposed participants had to have at least 6 months of GLP-1 RA treatment before the index date, follow-up did not coincide with treatment initiation, and many exposed individuals without a suitable match were excluded. These issues could materially affect the estimated association.",
