            "administered_framework_template": "rob_tools/robins_i/template.json",
            "rob_routing_status": "ready",
            "rob_routing_reason": "recommended RoB tool is implemented locally",
            "rob_routing_evidence_json": "[\"We conducted a target trial emulation using the TriNetX global health research network (> 120 healthcare organisations, > 200 million patients; January 2014\u2013June 2025).\", \"New users of GLP- 1RAs (liraglutide, semaglutide, tirzepatide) were compared with new users of other FDA- approved weight- loss medications (orlistat, phentermine- topiramate, bupropion- naltrexone, setmelanotide).\", \"Follow- up began at the index date with a minimum 6- month observation period and continued until first AMD occurrence, death or end of the observation period (30 June 2025).\"]",
            "rob_attempted": true,
            "assessment_status": "completed",
            "rob_skip_or_failure_reason": "",
            "overall_risk_of_bias": "serious",
            "overall_reason": "Overall risk of bias is judged serious for the incident any AMD result. The active-comparator new-user target-trial-emulation design, exclusion of prior AMD, propensity score matching, sensitivity analyses, and negative control outcomes reduce several biases. However, this remains a non-randomized EHR-based study with important residual confounding: after matching, BMI and HbA1c remained substantially imbalanced, and analyses restricted to HbA1c <5.7% or excluding prior antidiabetic medication use attenuated the AMD association. Additional concerns include differential follow-up duration, incomplete information on adherence/discontinuation and missing data handling, and outcome ascertainment by ICD-10-CM codes, including a large unspecified AMD component.",
            "Bias due to confounding": "serious",
            "Bias in selection of participants into the study": "moderate",
            "Bias in classification of interventions": "low",
            "Bias due to deviations from intended interventions": "moderate",
